nt tests.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","T9","necessity","project.portability"],"criteria":["T9"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.4475,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'create or strengthen the smallest in-process oracle that forces the lazy-import state'","Plan: 'forces a fresh Anthropic provider import after the monkeypatch'","T9(d): a RETIRE/cleanup path must exist so shared state doesn't leak/accumulate; forcing a fresh import mutates sys.modules and the monkeypatched attribute, which are process-global"],"finding":"The in-process oracle forces a fresh Anthropic provider import after the monkeypatch, mutating shared module-import state (sys.modules, the patched anthropic.AsyncAnthropic attribute, and the lazily-imported pydantic_ai.providers.anthropic module). The plan does not specify a RETIRE/cleanup path that restores this import state after the oracle, yet import-state leakage across tests is precisely the order-dependence mechanism under repair — an oracle that itself leaves mutated import state could re-introduce the same cross-test pollution it is meant to guard against.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7be93eb1686b22b9","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"AC: 'The in-process unit oracle forces a fresh Anthropic provider import after the monkeypatch...'","norm_id":"n35f8933e8d7ce075","priority":0.5025,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["If the oracle deletes/repopulates pydantic_ai.providers.anthropic in sys.modules without restoring it, a later test in the same process may observe the oracle's forced import state and fail or pass spuriously — recreating the order-dependence the ticket exists to eliminate."],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"State that the oracle uses a fixture/teardown (e.g. monkeypatch's automatic undo plus save/restore of sys.modules['pydantic_ai.providers.anthropic']) to reset import state, and add an assertion that a subsequent import is unaffected.","tier":"LLM","va